---
/**
 * The lightbox: one `<dialog>` per page, shared by every figure on it.
 *
 * Included once from `Base.astro`. Nothing here is per-image — the script
 * delegates from `document`, so a figure added later is lightboxable without
 * touching this file or being registered anywhere.
 *
 * ## Why a native `<dialog>` and not a library
 *
 * `showModal()` already provides the things a lightbox library is mostly for:
 * a top-layer element that cannot be scrolled behind, focus moved in and
 * restored on close, focus trapped while open, `Escape` to dismiss, and a real
 * `::backdrop` to paint. GLightbox and PhotoSwipe are 30-50 kB to reimplement
 * that in JavaScript.
 *
 * This site has deliberately kept its client-side JavaScript to the theme
 * toggle. That is an argued position — see `docs/manifesto/06-reversibility.md`
 * on the cost of a JavaScript surface — so a feature this small paying a
 * dependency for it would be the wrong trade.
 *
 * ## The trigger is a link, not a button
 *
 * "Opens a dialog" is normally a `<button>`. This uses an `<a>` pointing at the
 * image, and gains two things for it:
 *
 *   * **It works with JavaScript off.** The click falls through to the image
 *     file, which is a worse experience than a lightbox and a much better one
 *     than an inert cursor.
 *   * **Cmd-click and middle-click still open the raw capture in a new tab**,
 *     which is what somebody comparing two screenshots actually wants. The
 *     handler checks for modifier keys and gets out of the way.
 *
 * The cost is that a screen reader announces a link rather than a dialog
 * trigger, so `aria-label` on each trigger says "larger" explicitly.
 *
 * ## What it does not do
 *
 * There is no next/previous, no pinch-zoom and no panning. On a phone the
 * enlarged image is barely wider than the inline one — 96vw against a figure
 * that was already full-width — so "Enlarge" is close to an over-promise there.
 * What the lightbox still buys on a small screen is the surrounding page getting
 * out of the way, and a `frame="tall"` capture being shown *whole* rather than
 * cropped, which is why the badge on those says "Cropped — see all" instead.
 *
 * Panning a large image is the obvious next thing and is deliberately not here:
 * it needs gesture handling, and that is the point where this stops being forty
 * lines and starts being a library.
 *
 * Verified by `scripts/screenshots/lightbox-check.mjs`, which drives a real
 * browser against the built site — twenty assertions covering open, the four
 * ways of closing, the modifier-click fall-through, keyboard access, and that
 * the enlarged image is actually larger than the one in the page.
 */
---

{/*
  The dialog's own parts are addressed by `id`, and the `data-lightbox-*`
  namespace is reserved for data carried by a *trigger*. Both used `data-`
  attributes at first, and `[data-lightbox-caption]` then matched 27 elements:
  one caption sink in here, and 26 trigger links carrying the caption to put in
  it. Source and sink sharing a selector is a bug waiting for its first query.
*/}
<dialog id="ae-lightbox" class="ae-lightbox" aria-label="Enlarged screenshot">
  {/*
    The image and its caption sit in a wrapper so a click on the dialog itself —
    the area around them — can be told apart from a click on the content. That
    is how backdrop-dismissal is detected: `::backdrop` is not an element and
    never receives events, so `event.target === dialog` is the only signal.
  */}
  {/*
    Three rows, in this order, so nothing can overlap anything: the controls
    above, the image in the middle, the caption below.

    The first arrangement put the close button in a bar *under* the image, and
    the image covered it — Playwright reported the click being intercepted 55
    times before timing out. A flex column whose middle item is a large image is
    exactly where that goes wrong, and no z-index fixes it honestly: the button
    simply has to be somewhere the image cannot reach.
  */}
  <div class="ae-lightbox-frame">
    <div class="ae-lightbox-controls">
      <button type="button" id="ae-lightbox-close" class="ae-lightbox-close" aria-label="Close" title="Close (Esc)">
        <svg
          viewBox="0 0 24 24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2"
          stroke-linecap="round"
          aria-hidden="true"
        >
          <path d="M6 6l12 12M18 6L6 18"></path>
        </svg>
      </button>
    </div>

    <img id="ae-lightbox-image" alt="" decoding="async" />

    <p id="ae-lightbox-caption" class="ae-lightbox-caption"></p>
  </div>

</dialog>

<script is:inline>
  (() => {
    const dialog = document.getElementById('ae-lightbox');
    if (!dialog || typeof dialog.showModal !== 'function') return;

    const image = document.getElementById('ae-lightbox-image');
    const caption = document.getElementById('ae-lightbox-caption');

    // `showModal()` makes the page inert but does not stop it scrolling behind
    // the backdrop in every browser, so the scrollbar is taken out by hand and
    // the previous value restored rather than assumed to be `''`.
    let restoreOverflow = '';

    function open(trigger) {
      const src = trigger.dataset.lightboxSrc || trigger.getAttribute('href');
      if (!src) return;

      image.src = src;
      image.alt = trigger.dataset.lightboxAlt || '';

      const text = trigger.dataset.lightboxCaption || '';
      caption.textContent = text;
      caption.hidden = text === '';

      restoreOverflow = document.documentElement.style.overflow;
      document.documentElement.style.overflow = 'hidden';
      dialog.showModal();
    }

    document.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      const trigger = event.target.closest('[data-lightbox-src]');
      if (!trigger) return;

      // Let the browser have the ones where the user has asked for a new tab or
      // a download. Without this, cmd-click on a screenshot silently opens a
      // modal instead — the single most annoying thing a lightbox can do.
      if (event.button !== 0 || event.metaKey || event.ctrlKey || event.shiftKey || event.altKey) {
        return;
      }

      event.preventDefault();
      open(trigger);
    });

    // Dismiss on a click that lands on the dialog but outside its content.
    // `::backdrop` is a pseudo-element and receives no events at all, which is
    // why this compares against the dialog rather than listening on it.
    dialog.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
      if (event.target === dialog) dialog.close();
    });

    document.getElementById('ae-lightbox-close').addEventListener('click', () => dialog.close());

    // Fires for the close button, the backdrop, and Escape alike — Escape is
    // handled by the browser and never reaches a keydown listener here.
    dialog.addEventListener('close', () => {
      document.documentElement.style.overflow = restoreOverflow;
    });
  })();
</script>
